  {
   "type": "done",
   "answer": "An ideal M-stage pipeline needs **M + N – 1 cycles** to execute N instructions.\n\n**Reasoning and Calculation:**\nAccording to the lecture, a \"Perfect Pipeline\" is defined as one where one instruction completes per cycle after filling the pipeline, with no bubbles and maximum throughput. For a pipeline with M stages executing N instructions, the total number of cycles is calculated as:\n\n**Total Cycles = M + N – 1** [S1, p. 17]\n\n**Assumptions:**\nThe lecture specifies the following assumptions for this \"Perfect Pipeline\" scenario:\n1.  **No Bubbles:** There are no idle cycles in the pipeline.\n2.  **Maximum Throughput:** One instruction completes per cycle after the initial fill.\n3.  **Ideal Conditions:** The pipeline operates without stalls or hazards that would disrupt the flow.\n\n**Source:**\n*   [S1] Page 17: \"For N instructions and M stages: Total Cycles = M + N – 1\" and \"Perfect Pipeline: One instruction completes per cycle after filling the pipeline. No bubbles and maximum throughput.\"",
   "citations": [
    {
     "document_id": "34911c46e78edc833f283f8dd678b85d6f8c92d66859e6bf6886949181d95402",
     "chunk_id": "34911c46e78edc833f283f8dd678b85d6f8c92d66859e6bf6886949181d95402-p17-1",
     "page": 17,
     "text": "CPU: Optimize Latency of a Small Number of Complex Threads\n  Five-Stage Instruction Pipeline\n\n¡ Perfect Pipeline\n\n   ¡ One instruction completes per cycle after filling\n      the pipeline.\n\n   ¡ No bubbles and maximum throughput.\n\n\n¡ For N instructions and M stages:\n\n   ¡  Total Cycles = M + N – 1\n\n   ¡  Average throughput\n     = N / (M + N - 1) instructions per cycle\n\n   ¡  Instruction throughput per second\n     Frequency * N / (M + N - 1)",
     "preview_url": "/api/documents/34911c46e78edc833f283f8dd678b85d6f8c92d66859e6bf6886949181d95402/pages/17/preview",
     "source": "S1"
